//@ run-pass
//@ requires: madaros

// D17 trust-anchor-forgery guard (stdlib/x509/trust_store.sio,
// trust_store_has_matching_key_for_subject): before this task, this
// function's OWN separate guard rejected any candidate whose
// public_key_algorithm was neither PUBKEY_ALG_RSA nor PUBKEY_ALG_EC_P256
// -- meaning a P-384-keyed self-signed root could NEVER be recognized as
// a trusted anchor, even with a perfectly matching subject and key, and
// even after Task 5's separate x509_verify_signature dispatch fix. This
// test proves the guard now accepts PUBKEY_ALG_EC_P384 too, and that the
// original PUBKEY_ALG_EC_P256 case still works unchanged.
//
// Both DER fixtures are real openssl-generated self-signed certificates
// reused verbatim from existing tests in this repo (the DER bytes,
// lengths and generation commands are unchanged from their source
// files):
//   - P-384: tests/run-pass/x509_parse_p384_public_key.sio (Task 4's own
//     fixture, CN=p384-test.example, subject == issuer)
//   - P-256: tests/run-pass/x509_ec_public_key_parsing.sio (CN=ec-test.example,
//     subject == issuer)
// Each is self-signed, so parsing the SAME DER twice into "the trust
// store's stored root" and "the candidate cert being checked" gives an
// exact subject+key match by construction -- the minimal real proof that
// the guard change (not just the constant identity) is what lets the
// match through.

use x509::cert::*
use x509::trust_store::*
use bignum::bigint::*
use net::socket::*

